      "writtenOn": "2023-02-16T06:23:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I\u0027m not sure about the lifetime issues either, but I think if possible the constructor interface should be consistent, i.e., if a GraphitePipelineDesc takes an ID then the ComputePipelineDesc should, or both should take corresponding \u0027Step\u0027 pointer.\n\nThe constructor interfaces are already consistent because `GraphicsPipelineDesc` defines a `const RenderStep*` parameter. The only difference is that `ComputePipelineDesc` caches the pointer while `GraphicsPipelineDesc` caches the step\u0027s ID.\n\n\u003e Originally GraphicsPipelineDesc just held on to a RenderStep* since the lifetime was guaranteed to outlive the description.\n\u003e \n\u003e That lifetime guarantee still exists for RenderSteps since they live as long as the SHaredContext. If ComputeSteps could have the same guarantee it could also just stay as a pointer.\n\u003e \n\u003e One of the main reasons for switching to an id from a pointer was to keep the pipeline description compact. GraphicsPipelineDesc is just 64 bits now instead of 128 (including alignment padding). It certainly complicates look up since you have to be able to get to the RendererProvider in order to generate the SkSL, but that is not a performance critical path so I figured it was worth while. Having the description be just integer types makes it easier to write dependent keys as well, since caps have to combine them into a final render pass key.\n\u003e \n\u003e It\u0027s valid to cast the RenderStep* or ComputeStep* to a uintptr_t and use that in the key but then all of our keys get 4 bytes bigger.\n\nThat makes sense. FWIW the ComputePipeline logic also uses the ComputeStep\u0027s 32-bit unique ID as the key, so at least that part of the logic is the same. `ComputePipelineDesc` currently doesn\u0027t have any fields apart from the `ComputeStep` pointer. I think in the future the workgroup size itself may become a factor in the key but itself can be obtained from the `ComputeStep`.\n\nGiven that `ComputePipelineDesc` consists only of a pointer I think it should be just as compact. At least the ease of dependency injection for testability is a benefit.",
